WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:06.231
One aircraft crossed the Atlantic in under three and a half hours, half the time of

00:06.231 --> 00:11.727
an ordinary jet. This is Concorde, the supersonic airliner that redefined flying,

00:11.727 --> 00:13.016
and why it stopped.

00:13.916 --> 00:20.175
Concorde cruised at Mach 2.02, more than twice the speed of sound, at sixty thousand

00:20.175 --> 00:24.497
feet, powered by four Olympus turbojets with afterburners.

00:25.397 --> 00:29.651
Its slender delta wing gave lift at both low and high speed.

00:29.651 --> 00:35.324
The nose drooped for takeoff and landing, then rose flush as friction heated the

00:35.324 --> 00:37.451
skin during supersonic cruise.

00:38.351 --> 00:42.269
Concorde never became the commercial hit its makers hoped.

00:42.269 --> 00:47.741
Its sonic boom banned supersonic flight over land, costs spiralled into billions,

00:47.741 --> 00:50.511
and only twenty aircraft were ever built.

00:51.411 --> 00:58.437
That safety record ended on 25 July 2000, when Air France Flight 4590 crashed after

00:58.437 --> 01:03.346
takeoff, killing 109 people aboard and four on the ground.

01:03.346 --> 01:10.118
Falling passenger numbers and soaring costs after that sealed its fate; Concorde

01:10.118 --> 01:12.573
flew its last flight in 2003.

01:13.473 --> 01:18.879
Eighteen of the twenty Concordes built survive in museums today, monuments to a

01:18.879 --> 01:24.353
plane that halved Atlantic crossing times and captured the public's imagination.

01:24.353 --> 01:29.828
Voted Britain's greatest design, it remains the only supersonic airliner to have

01:29.828 --> 01:31.607
carried paying passengers.
